40
00:04:43.629 --> 00:04:51.069
Luca | Vero: My personal opinion is the separate endpoint is much… more, elegant, simple, Teresa?

41
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.890
Justin Traglia: Alright, how do others feel about this?

42
00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:04.239
Justin Traglia: Do clients have opinions on whether it should be separate or… The existing one, Nico?

43
00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:13.620
Nico Flaig: Yeah, there's just… so we made the builder-config required in Produce Block before, so maybe that's an argument for having it separate also.

44
00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:25.630
Nico Flaig: But besides that one thing, so how does that work if you weight the bit that Vero selects, for example, against the local payload?

45
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.229
Nico Flaig: Right, you would pass a boost factor to the Beacon node, I think, right?

46
00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.329
Luca | Vero: I'm not sure if there's a boost factor right now, but…

47
00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:43.299
Luca | Vero: If there would be, you would just, yeah, boost the local value, and if it's higher, then you could override it.

48
00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:49.300
Nico Flaig: Yeah, so…

49
00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.040
Luca | Vero: So it's.

50
00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:50.490
Nico Flaig: the…

51
00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:52.660
Luca | Vero: Makes sense for this endpoint, actually.

52
00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:57.490
Nico Flaig: But you select a bit, right, and then you pass it…

53
00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:03.080
Nico Flaig: to the Beacon, how would you ever pick a local payload in that case?

54
00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:09.740
Luca | Vero: You wouldn't. The only time you would expect… a local payload?

55
00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:16.030
Luca | Vero: is if the circuit breaker is active, or something like that. In that case, the Beaconaut can use the local one.

56
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:24.189
Luca | Vero: But if you are bringing your own bid, then you expect to include that bit in a blog in most cases.

57
00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:36.859
Nico Flaig: Yeah, I'm thinking functionality-wise, you should probably pass the boost factor, or the… maybe it's enough if the Beacon node applies some default, yeah.

58
00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:47.950
Nico Flaig: I guess I'm not sure, is anyone gonna implement that, besides, like, Vero or Vouch? Maybe Nimbus? I'm not sure.

59
00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:56.450
Nico Flaig: I think there are not many valid clients that behave like this, where they query all connected Beacon nodes.

60
00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.060
Dustin: Interesting question, we haven't really…

61
00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:10.199
Dustin: approach this, but obviously Nimbus does, in general, have that behavior, and might exploit that.

62
00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.640
Luca | Vero: The question is more about if…

63
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:22.720
Luca | Vero: The Validator client wants to, fetch bits directly instead of letting the Beacon do it.

64
00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:25.640
Dustin: Yep.

65
00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:33.839
Dustin: So far, Nimbus has not tended to have that approach. It's a pretty… it's much more, kind of, lightweight.

66
00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:34.810
Dustin: Thing.

67
00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:49.669
Chris Berry: Just on this, so how do we intend to switch between trusted and trustless bids from the multi-client side?

68
00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:58.329
Chris Berry: Because if we're getting bids from the relays, and we propose a bid, dire, however it works, whichever endpoint we use.

69
00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:06.909
Chris Berry: How do we know that the trustless bids are not better than the MEV one that we've received via the relays? And who makes that decision?

70
00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:16.689
Luca | Vero: Well, as a Validator client, you can subscribe to the trustless bids over SSE, so that's one way

71
00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:19.029
Luca | Vero: To approach this.

72
00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:24.699
Chris Berry: Right, okay, so you're basically in a world where you're gonna ignore…

73
00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:38.339
Chris Berry: the local block, whatever it's worth, and it's up to the Validator client to differentiate between anything that comes over the gossip Trust Plus path, or the Web2 MEV Relay path.

74
00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:43.729
Chris Berry: And essentially offer up one to the… To the Beacon node.

75
00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:45.489
Chris Berry: That makes sense.

76
00:08:49.420 --> 00:09:00.900
Nico Flaig: Yeah, but I think that's another detail, maybe, worth to define on the spec in your PR Luca, because it's not clear whether the beacon node should bring in the P2P bit, or…

77
00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:07.519
Nico Flaig: Like, we expect the Validator client to consider them by using the SSE event instead.

78
00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.409
Luca | Vero: Yeah, that makes sense, I'll edit, I think.

79
00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.230
Luca | Vero: In this case, the Validator client should take those trusted bits into account as well.

80
00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:38.119
Chris Berry: Because it's… it really is… it really is dependent on who is deciding which to choose. If we take the path that the… the Validator client knows what it's doing, and whatever it sends up, that's what you have to choose. Ignore any local, ignore anything else, that's what's going to be used, then yeah, fine, you don't need to add any kind of…

81
00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:44.030
Chris Berry: Boost factor or something similar to help guide the Beacon node to make the decision you want.

82
00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:48.180
Chris Berry: And that works, I think that's a reasonable path to take, it's just that

83
00:09:48.380 --> 00:10:00.639
Chris Berry: as Nico said, we should be explicit that that's the intended behavior, that you're basically gonna throw away any decision-making from the Beacon node side, and it's basically gonna take what the Validator client offers up.

84
00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:11.849
Chris Berry: And ignore the rest. I think that… it makes sense in that use case. It's just a shame that you can't really consider the local block, but I think in today's world, you can't really consider the local block either.

85
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:25.319
Chris Berry: well, it's something that happens on the Beacon node side, right? It looks at what comes on the external builder endpoint, and obviously could build a local block, but I don't think any decision making is made today. I think if one's on the builder endpoint, it is what's selected.

86
00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:36.210
Luca | Vero: Yeah, unless we would want the local payloads also to be broadcast over SSE or something like that.

87
00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:48.520
Chris Berry: I don't know who would do that, because presumably right now, that's only done if a valid proposer asks the Beacon node to build them a blog.

88
00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:53.970
Luca | Vero: Yeah.

89
00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:58.049
Luca | Vero: Well, maybe one more thing is, since…

90
00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:06.720
Luca | Vero: The bit that the Validator client brings could be invalid on one of the Beacon nodes it's connected to, because it's…

91
00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.080
Luca | Vero: On a different head, or something like that.

92
00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:13.800
Luca | Vero: So, it may be good to clarify what the Beaconode should do in that case.

93
00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:33.710
Chris Berry: I mean, that's a good point, right? Because, like, what we just talked about, offering up a bid, and that's what the Beacon node takes regardless, you basically say you're not allowed to do a check, right? But I think it does make sense to say, hey, if this is an invalid block in our view, in the Beacon node's view, throw it away and build a local one.

94
00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:38.020
Chris Berry: But, oh, hang on a minute, you can't do that because you've already signed the blog.

95
00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:39.349
Chris Berry: At this stage.

96
00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:48.710
Luca | Vero: Yeah, no, I think the BR actually says something like, if the Beacon considers the bid invalid, it should disregard it.

97
00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:54.359
Chris Berry: Right, but then it can't replace it with a local block, right? It's just, you've missed the slot.

98
00:11:57.130 --> 00:11:58.240
Luca | Vero: Well, that's gone.

99
00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.849
Chris Berry: Because you've already signed the block at that stage.

100
00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:01.979
Chris Berry: Have you not?

101
00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:03.310
Luca | Vero: I don't think so.

102
00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:06.919
Luca | Vero: This is during the Block production, before the signing.

103
00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:15.469
Chris Berry: Right, so we take the bid, we offer it to the Beacon node, then the Beacon node returns a block with that.

104
00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.829
Chris Berry: A proposal to sign based on that.

105
00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:27.080
Luca | Vero: Yeah, but before the Beaconaut returns the block, it can validate the bit, and if it's not valid in its…

106
00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:31.190
Luca | Vero: viewed, and it can return a block with a different bin.

107
00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:45.360
Chris Berry: Okay. Well, as long as that's the behavior of the PR, then great, that works well, so it could actually ignore the… whatever the validator selects and send back a local one, and that's because it's failed validation. That makes sense, if that's how it works.

108
00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:51.950
Luca | Vero: Yeah, that's how I imagine it would work. It has to have some leeway, too.

109
00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.109
Luca | Vero: Replace it if necessary.

110
00:12:56.650 --> 00:13:06.289
Nico Flaig: I think one more concern could be also the timing of the API call, because I'm guessing you will not call this at the start of the slot right away, right?

111
00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:09.139
Luca | Vero: Right, yeah.

112
00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:26.920
Nico Flaig: And, at least how CL clients behave right now, they will only start building the CL block site, or most clients do this. They start this only when the API is called to produce a block. So if that is laid on the slot, that could be potentially problematic.

113
00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:32.860
Luca | Vero: Good point.

114
00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:36.690
Luca | Vero: There's no easy way to solve that, right?

115
00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:54.149
Chris Berry: Well, I think you just have to control it on the Validator side, right? And just make sure you have a deadline of something sensible, and then you definitely call the API within that time to make sure that it's…

116
00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:57.860
Chris Berry: got enough. I don't know if we have an idea of how long that is. I think…

117
00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:04.820
Chris Berry: I think on production today, we generally make that… our deadline is about one and a half seconds into the slot, I think.

118
00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.900
Chris Berry: I don't know if that would need to change for this.

119
00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:20.950
Luca | Vero: Yeah. Do we have any idea if the time that it takes to produce a block is dominated by the EL payload, or is it the attestation packing, or…

120
00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.600
Luca | Vero: Because obviously, yeah.

121
00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:28.240
Nico Flaig: Yeah, I think the packing is probably the bottleneck here.

122
00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:34.640
Nico Flaig: at least there's some worst cases, at least in Lodestar, where it can take, like, 800 MS.

123
00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:37.980
Nico Flaig: I think Teku mentioned something earlier about this, too.

124
00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:46.060
Luca | Vero: Okay, but I think this is not such a huge deal, because it's… It's…

125
00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:53.269
Luca | Vero: kind of an advanced Beacon API endpoint, so the callers will just need to make sure that they're calling it on time.

126
00:14:55.290 --> 00:15:05.760
Nico Flaig: Well, what was the issue of you just calling this on every Beacon node, and you just, sell, like, just get the local payload that the Beacon node produces?

127
00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:10.699
Nico Flaig: And then, only Vero calls the builders.

128
00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:14.639
Luca | Vero: Oh, that you replace the bid directly in that block that is returned?

129
00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:18.070
Nico Flaig: Yeah, and then you… Let me see…

130
00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:21.949
Luca | Vero: It affected the block's state rule, so that didn't work.

131
00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:27.190
Nico Flaig: Alright, and also it would not work with the circuit breaker.

132
00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:47.219
Chris Berry: Is the circuit breaker designed to be within the Beacon node, such that if we call it with, whatever, a MEV bid, but the circuit breaker's on, then we always get back the local block instead, regardless of how valid the bid is?

133
00:15:49.360 --> 00:16:05.620
Nico Flaig: Yeah, I mean, that should be on the Beacon node side, I think. There's also, another factor, which is, we have this EL flag today, which says, like, should override builder, which is, if the EL detects censorship, it will also use the local payload.

134
00:16:07.910 --> 00:16:15.160
Nico Flaig: But yeah, I think it's hard for you guys to implement a circuit breaker, because you need the full review of fork choice and stuff, so…

135
00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:32.179
Chris Berry: Yeah, I think, as it is today, we should retain this logic where it's the Beacon node giving the validator client which block to use, and then circuit breakers, validity, everything else can remain in the beacon node.

136
00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:44.239
Chris Berry: And it's always… then it's a simple path for the Validator client once we get something back from the Beacon nodes themselves, that it's basically whatever we get, we sign it, we submit it, and that's… that's kind of done.

137
00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:53.609
Chris Berry: So it would make sense in my head to preserve that, that everything… essentially, the last steps for the Validator client are, whatever you get back, sign it and send it.

138
00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:04.730
Luca | Vero: Yep, agreed on my side as well.

139
00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:14.460
Luca | Vero: So, yeah, like I said, I'll update the PR today, and I think we can move on to the next topic.

